t leading-tight line-clamp-2 md:line-clamp-1 pointer-events-none">Observati=
on and Imagination in The Starry Night (1889)</span>
        <span class=3D"flex items-center h-[1.125rem] shrink-0 pointer-even=
ts-none">
          <svg xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width=3D"16" height=3D"=
16" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" fill=3D"none" class=3D"size-5 md:size-4 transitio=
n-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180">
  <path d=3D"M13 5.5L8 10.5L3 5.5" stroke=3D"currentColor" stroke-width=3D"=
3"></path>
</svg>
        </span>
      </summary>
      <div id=3D"expandable-text-panel-0" class=3D"pt-4">
        <div id=3D"expandable-text-content-0" class=3D"text-sm leading-body=
-sm md:text-md md:leading-body [&amp;_p+p]:mt-4 [&amp;_a]:underline [&amp;_=
em]:italic">
            <p>=E2=80=9CThis morning I saw the countryside from my window a=
 long time before sunrise, with nothing but the morning star, which looked =
very big,=E2=80=9D wrote van Gogh to his brother Theo, describing his inspi=
ration for one of his best-known paintings, <em>The Starry Night</em>. The =
window to which he refers was in the Saint-Paul asylum in Saint-R=C3=A9my, =
in southern France, where he sought respite from his emotional suffering wh=
ile continuing to make art.<br><br>This mid-scale, oil-on-<a href=3D"https:=
//www.moma.org/collection/terms/canvas">canvas</a> painting is dominated by=
 a moon- and star-filled night sky. It takes up three-quarters of the pictu=
re plane and appears turbulent, even agitated, with intensely swirling patt=
erns that seem to roll across its surface like waves. It is pocked with bri=
ght orbs=E2=80=94including the crescent moon to the far right, and Venus, t=
he morning star, to the left of center=E2=80=94surrounded by concentric cir=
cles of radiant white and yellow light.<br><br>Beneath this expressive sky =
sits a hushed village of humble houses surrounding a church, whose steeple =
rises sharply above the undulating blue-black mountains in the background. =
A cypress tree sits at the foreground of this night scene. Flame-like, it r=
eaches almost to the top edge of the canvas, serving as a visual link betwe=
en land and sky. Considered symbolically, the cypress could be seen as a br=
idge between life, as represented by the earth, and death, as represented b=
y the sky, commonly associated with heaven. Cypresses were also regarded as=
 trees of the graveyard and mourning. =E2=80=9CBut the sight of the stars a=
lways makes me dream,=E2=80=9D van Gogh once wrote. =E2=80=9CWhy, I say to =
myself, should the spots of light in the firmament be less accessible to us=
 than the black spots on the map of France? Just as we take the train to go=
 to Tarascon or Rouen, we take death to go to a star.=E2=80=9D<br><br><em>T=
he Starry Night</em> is based on van Gogh=E2=80=99s direct observations as =
well as his imagination, memories, and emotions. The steeple of the church,=
 for example, resembles those common in his native Holland, not in France. =
The whirling <a href=3D"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/form">forms</=
a> in the sky, on the other hand, match published astronomical observations=
 of clouds of dust and gas known as nebulae. At once balanced and expressiv=
e, the composition is structured by his ordered placement of the cypress, s=
teeple, and central nebulae, while his countless short brushstrokes and thi=
ckly applied paint set its surface in roiling motion. Such a combination of=
 visual contrasts was generated by an artist who found beauty and interest =
in the night, which, for him, was =E2=80=9Cmuch more alive and richly color=
ed than the day.=E2=80=9D</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details id=3D"expandable-text-1" class=3D"group">
