t leading-tight line-clamp-2 md:line-clamp-1 pointer-events-none">Vincent v=
an Gogh: Emotion, Vision, and A Singular Style</span>
        <span class=3D"flex items-center h-[1.125rem] shrink-0 pointer-even=
ts-none">
          <svg xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width=3D"16" height=3D"=
16" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" fill=3D"none" class=3D"size-5 md:size-4 transitio=
n-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180">
  <path d=3D"M13 5.5L8 10.5L3 5.5" stroke=3D"currentColor" stroke-width=3D"=
3"></path>
</svg>
        </span>
      </summary>
      <div id=3D"expandable-text-panel-1" class=3D"pt-4">
        <div id=3D"expandable-text-content-1" class=3D"text-sm leading-body=
-sm md:text-md md:leading-body [&amp;_p+p]:mt-4 [&amp;_a]:underline [&amp;_=
em]:italic">
            <p>Mention Vincent van Gogh and one of the first things likely =
to come to many people=E2=80=99s minds is the fact that he cut off his own =
ear. This stark act, committed in 1888, marked the beginning of the depress=
ion that would continue until the end of his life. But to know van Gogh is =
to get past the caricature of the tortured, misunderstood artist and to bec=
ome acquainted instead with the hardworking, deeply religious, and difficul=
t man. Van Gogh found his place in art and produced emotional, visually arr=
esting <a href=3D"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/painting">paintings=
</a> over the course of a career that lasted only a decade.<br><br>Largely =
self-taught, van Gogh produced more than 2,000 <a href=3D"https://www.moma.=
org/collection/terms/oil-paint">oil-paintings</a>, <a href=3D"https://www.m=
oma.org/collection/terms/watercolor">watercolors</a>, <a href=3D"https://ww=
w.moma.org/collection/terms/drawing">drawings</a>, and <a href=3D"https://w=
ww.moma.org/collection/terms/sketch">sketches</a>, which became in demand o=
nly after his death. He also wrote scores of letters, especially to his bro=
ther Theo, in which he worked out his thoughts about art. =E2=80=9CAlways c=
ontinue walking a lot and loving nature, for that=E2=80=99s the real way to=
 learn to understand art better and better,=E2=80=9D he wrote in 1874. =E2=
=80=9CPainters understand nature and love it, and <em>teach us to see</em>.=
=E2=80=9D<br><br>It was nature, and the people living closely to it, that f=
irst stirred van Gogh=E2=80=99s artistic inclinations. In this he was not a=
lone. <a href=3D"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/landscape">Landscape=
s</a> remained a popular subject in late-nineteenth-century art. Driven in =
part by their dissatisfaction with the modern city, many artists sought out=
 places resembling earthly paradises, where they could observe nature first=
hand, feeding its psychological and spiritual resonances into their work. V=
an Gogh was particularly taken with the peasants he saw working the country=
side; his early compositions featured <a href=3D"https://www.moma.org/colle=
ction/terms/portrait">portraits</a> of Dutch peasants and rural landscapes,=
 rendered in dark, moody <a href=3D"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/t=
one">tones</a>.<br><br>In 1886, van Gogh moved to Paris, where he encounter=
ed the works of the <a href=3D"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/impres=
sionism">Impressionists</a> and <a href=3D"https://www.moma.org/collection/=
terms/neo-impressionism">Neo-Impressionists</a>, and the <a href=3D"https:/=
/www.moma.org/collection/terms/pointillism">Pointillist</a> compositions of=
 Georges Seurat. Inspired by these artists=E2=80=99 harmonious matching of =
colors, shorter brushstrokes, and liberal use of paint, he brightened his o=
wn palette and loosened his brushwork, emphasizing the physical application=
 of paint on the canvas. The style he developed in Paris and carried throug=
h to the end of his life became known as <a href=3D"https://www.moma.org/co=
llection/terms/post-impressionism">Post-Impressionism</a>, a term encompass=
ing works made by artists unified by their interest in expressing their emo=
tional and psychological responses to the world through bold colors and <a =
href=3D"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/expressionism">expressive</a>=
, often symbolic images. In a letter to his sister Willemien, touching upon=
 the mind and temperament of artists, van Gogh once wrote that he was =E2=
=80=9Cvery sensitive to color and its particular language, its effects of c=
omplementaries, contrasts, harmony.=E2=80=9D<br><br>By 1888, van Gogh had r=
eturned to the French countryside, where he would remain until his death. T=
here, close once again to the peasants who had inspired him early on, he co=
ncentrated on painting landscapes, portraits (of himself and others), domes=
tic interiors, and <a href=3D"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/still-l=
ife">still lifes</a> full of personal symbolism.</p>
